### [RootViz: visualizing real-time monitoring of the Root DNS Servers](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iepg-sessa-rootviz-visualizing-real-time-monitoring-of-the-root-dns-servers-00)
* **Presenter:** Giovane Moura (SIDN Labs / TU Delft)
* **Overview:**
  * Introduced RootViz, a real-time dashboard aggregating RIPE Atlas active measurement data (14,000+ probes across 180 countries) to monitor the reachability and performance of all 13 root server letters.
  * Emphasized that for root DNS servers, reachability is the primary metric, while latency is secondary due to aggressive caching by recursive resolvers.
* **Findings:**
  * Root server instances maintain distinct baseline failure rates across global networks.
  * Spikes in probe timeouts are frequently caused by transit or peering disputes in intermediate networks (e.g., between Deutsche Telekom and Cogent affecting C-Root reachability in Europe) rather than root infrastructure failures.
  * The administrative and operational diversity of the root server system ensures global DNS resilience; failures on individual root letters or routing paths do not impact end-user resolution because resolvers automatically fall back to alternative root letters.
  * Ongoing student research at TU Delft focuses on automating anomaly detection, analyzing BGP path shifts, and releasing public APIs and datasets for open source use.
* **Discussion:**
  * Joel Jaeggli inquired whether the observed events demonstrate the value of administrative diversity. Giovane Moura confirmed that the root system has never experienced a simultaneous global failure across all root letters, validating its design.

