### [The 2024 KSK Roll](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iepg-sessa-the-2024-ksk-roll-00)
* **Presenter:** Geoff Huston (APNIC)
* **Overview:**
  * Presented an evaluation of the October 11, 2024 Root Zone Key Signing Key (KSK) rollover using large-scale Google Ad-based end-user measurement.
  * Approximately 40% of global internet users currently validate DNSSEC.
* **Findings:**
  * The overall rollover executed smoothly without global network disruptions.
  * Pre-roll direct outreach to large service providers (such as Reliance Jio in India) successfully prevented outages and resulted in increased post-roll DNSSEC validation rates in those regions.
  * Networks where operators could not be contacted prior to the roll (e.g., Telenor in Bulgaria, operators in Brazil) experienced drops in validation as resolvers failed to update their trusted KSK states.
  * Critically evaluated trust anchor signaling mechanisms (RFC 8145 and RFC 8509 / Key Sentinel). Once a resolver fails validation, Key Sentinel signaling breaks down into noisy `SERVFAIL` responses rather than clear state reports, because resolvers execute sentinel checks late in the resolution stack rather than at the entry point.
  * Raised concerns regarding planned 3-year KSK rollover cycles and future post-quantum algorithm transitions, which will significantly increase signature sizes and force DNS over TCP.
* **Discussion & Chat:**
  * Andrew Campling raised questions regarding the 3-year rollover policy and post-quantum readiness. Geoff Huston noted that post-quantum keys will necessitate larger payloads and heavier reliance on TCP, which front-end load balancing infrastructure is currently ill-equipped to handle efficiently.
  * Dan York suggested establishing a formal, proactive outreach framework within ICANN/PTI to identify non-updated networks ahead of future key rolls.
  * Chat discussions (Warren Kumari, Erik Nygren, Tom Hill, David Lawrence) noted that several operators temporarily or permanently disabled DNSSEC validation in response to rollover failures rather than fixing local resolver trust anchors.

