### [Query Duplication in the DNS](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iepg-sessa-query-duplication-in-the-dns-00)
* **Presenter:** Geoff Huston (APNIC)
* **Overview:**
  * Analyzed duplicate query behavior at authoritative DNS servers using end-user ad traffic measurements.
* **Findings:**
  * Average queries generated per user request rose from 2.37 in 2019 to 4.43 in 2024.
  * Contributing factors include dual-stack IPv4/IPv6 querying (A and AAAA), increased usage of SVCB/HTTPS records (RFC 9460), and aggressive load balancer front-ends.
  * Load balancers and recursive clusters frequently replicate UDP queries across multiple back-end instances within 10ms to optimize for low latency, generating high rates of redundant traffic.
  * **Response Code Amplification:** Returning `SERVFAIL` acts as a severe traffic amplifier, generating an average of ~83 retry queries per request because resolvers interpret `SERVFAIL` as a transient, server-side error and retry across all configured upstreams. In contrast, returning `NXDOMAIN`, `REFUSED`, or `NOERROR` with `NODATA` yields dramatically lower retry rates.
* **Discussion & Chat:**
  * Rich Salz and David Lawrence noted that `SERVFAIL` carries the connotation of a transient server error, making retries expected resolver behavior.
  * Mark Andrews explained that stub resolvers hit all configured servers on `SERVFAIL` because the response is uncacheable at the stub level.
  * Erik Nygren suggested implementing exponential back-off in stubs and recursives upon receiving `SERVFAIL`.
  * Andrew Campling and Joel Jaeggli discussed whether a Best Current Practice (BCP) document is needed for DNS front-end load balancer implementations to curb unnecessary query duplication.

